X Way in the End-user Markets Business Line, as they were previously shown among Holding, Services and Other. 185 Principal Markets and Competition The ENEL Group is the world’s largest private operator of renewables in terms of installed capacity (about 80.8 GW in as of 30 September 2024); world leader among private-sector operators of distribution networks in terms of customers served (some 69.2 million as of 30 September 2024); world leader among private-sector operators in terms of retail power and gas customers (about 55.4 million – electricity and gas); and owns approximately 9.1 GW as of 30 September 2024 of demand response managed worldwide2. As of 31 December 202 3, ENEL was the principal electricity company in Italy (in terms of market share) by net electricity production ( 14%), end users ( 85%) and free retail customers ( 40.1%8). ENEL was also the principal electricity company in Spain (in terms of market share) by net electricity production ( 18.3%), electricity distributed (44.1%) and free retail customers (29%). According to the ENEL Group’s estimates based on data published in the financial statements of the main market operators, ENEL estimates that it is the second largest electricity company in Europe, based on total installed capacity. The ENEL Group’s ne t electricity production on 30 September 2024 amounted to 147.2 TWh, of which 27.0 TWh was produced in Italy, and 120.3 TWh was produ ced abroad, compared to 158 TW on 30 September 2023, of which 33.4 TWh was produced in Italy and 124.9 TWh was produced abroad. As of 30 September 2024, the Group conveyed 363.3 TWh of electricity through the grid, of which 163.0 TWh was in Italy and 200.3 TWh abroad, compared to 369.2 TWh of electricity as of 30 September 2023, of which 160.2 TWh was in Italy and 208.9 TWh abroad. As at 30 September 2024, the Group sold 5.0 billion cubic metres of gas, of which 2,4 billion cubic metres were sold in Italy, where, according to the Group’s estimates, the Group is the second largest operator, and 2.6 billion cubic metres were sold abroad, compared to 6.0 billion cubic metres of gas sold as at 30 September 2023 of which 2.9 billion cubic metres were sold in Italy, and 3.1 billion cubic metres were sold abroad. The following subsections describe the competitive position of the Group in each country/region in which it operates and set forth certain summary information regarding the regulatory systems in those countries. For further details, please make reference to “Regulation” below. Italy The Italian Electricity Market According to ENEL’s estimates, ENEL is the principal electricity producer in Italy, with 26.0 GW of installed capacity as of 30 September 2024. Its main competitors are Edison S.p.A., ENI S.p.A, A2A and EPH. According to the Italian Regulatory Authority for Energy, Networks and Environment (“ ARERA”), energy consumption in the Italian free market in 202 3 reached approximately 219 TWh excluding 5 .1 TWh of safeguard and 3.1 TWh of “tutele graduali”), compared to 223 TWh in 2022 (excluding 4.8 TWh of safeguard and 2.3 TWh of “tutele graduali”). As of 30 September 2024, ENEL sold electricity to 11,914,139.00 clients on the free market, of which 376,259 were business to business clients and11,537,880 were business to consumer clients, compared to 11,973,826 in the same period of 202 3, of which 447,451 were business to business clients and 11,526,375 were business to consumer clients. Of the total volume sold on the unregulated market, 32.0 TWh of electricity were sold to 2 Enel’s leadership in the different categories is defined by comparison with competitors’ FY2022 data. Fully state owned operators are not included. 8 Enel estimate based on 2023 closure, % calculated on regulated or free market (does not include “Safeguard,” includes “Gradua l Protection”); volumes gross loss, net self-consumption. 186 business-to-business clients (including 0.8 TWh of safeguard) and 18.5 TWh were sold to business to consumer clients as of 30 September 2024, compared to 37.1 TWh of electricity sold to business -to-business clients (including 1.4 TWh of safeguard) and 20.2 TWh sold to business to consumer clients in the same period of 2023. According to the ARERA, 2023 energy consumption on the regulated market amounted to approximately 14.4 TWh, compared to approximately 21.9 TWh net of network losses in 2022. As of 30 September 2024, ENEL sold 6.2 TWh to 2.9 million clients on the regulated market, compared to 9.3 TWh sold to 7.2 million clients in 2023. The Italian Natural Gas Market In the retail market, as of 30 September 2024, ENEL sold 0.7 billion cubic metres of gas to business-to-business clients and 1.7 billion cubic metres of gas to business to consumer clients, compared to 0.8 billion cubic metres of gas sold to business-to-business clients and2.1 billion cubic metres of gas sold to business to consumer clients in the same period of 2023. Iberia The Group’s installed capacity in Spain and Portugal amounted to 21.4 GW as of 30 September 2024. As of 30 September 2024, its production amounted to 45.7 TWh of energy and its sales amounted to 56.5 TWh, compared to 46 TWh of energy produced and 59.5 TWh sold as of 30 September 2023. The main operators in the Iberian electricity industry are Endesa, Iberdrola, EDP and Naturgy. Latin America As of 30 September 2024, the Group’s installed capacity in South America was equal to 21.7 GW, compared to 23.1 GW as of 31 December 2023. As of 30 September 2024, production amounted to 53.3 TWh and sales to final customers amounted to 95.6 TWh, compared to 56.8 TWh produced and 96.4 TWh sold to final customers at the same period of 2023. This region has reacted relatively well to the current economic crisis, due to the success of economic policies implemented in recent years. With respect to the electricity market, there is a need to increase generation capacity. The expectations for the development of the markets in this region, in terms of medium-and long-term growth in electricity demand and sales are sufficient to justify continued investment in the region. As of 30 September 2024, the Group had a total installed capacity in Argentina of 1.3 GW, compared to 1.3 GW from 202 3. As of 31 December 202 3, it held a n 2.8% share of the electricity generation market. In the distribution sector, the Group controls Edesur (Buenos Aires), a company with 2.7 million clients that distributed 13.5 TWh of energy as of 30 September 2024. As of 30 September 2024, the Group had a total installed capacity in Brazil of 6.6 GW, compared to 5.7 GW in 2023. As of 31 December 2023, it held approximately a 2.2% share of the electricity generation market. In the distribution sector, the Group controls Rio de Janeiro -based Ampla Investimentos (“ Ampla”), which has 3.1 million clients, Coelce, which has 4.2 million clients and Eletropaulo Metropolitana Eletricidade De Sao Paulo S.A. which has 8.5 million clients. Total energy distributed as of 30 September 2024 was 54.8 TWh, compared to 51.2 TWh as of 30 September 2023. As of 30 September 2024, the Group had a total installed capacity in Chile of 8.5 GW, compared to 8.8 GW in 2023. As of 31 December 2023, it held approximately a 28.8% share of the electricity generation market. In the distribution sector, the Group serves 2.2 million clients and distributed 11.1 TWh of energy as of 30 September 2024. 187 As of 30 September 2024, the Group had a total installed capacity in Colombia of 4.2 GW, compared to 3.9 in 2023. As of 31 December 202 3, it held a 19.8% share of the electricity generation market. In the distribution sector, ENEL controls Codensa (Bogotá), a company that has 3.9 million clients and distributed 11.5 TWh of energy as of 30 September 2024. As of 30 September 2024, the Group had a total installed capacity in Peru of 0.3 GW, compared to 2.4 GW in 2023. As of 31 December 2023, it held approximately a 19% share in the electricity generation market. Renewable Energy markets Within the renewable energy sector, which will continue to have an important role in ENEL’s future, the growth of the Group will be executed through the technologies described herein. Renewable energies confirm their trend of growth in all the technologies and geographical areas and, as in developing countries, such growth is usually associated with economic growth and does not derive solely from compliance with environmental protection regulations. The model of development of the renewable energies in ENEL is based on a diversified approach from both a technological and geographical perspective which is possible because of the flexibility in the allocation of investments which are approved on the basis of profitability only. In many countries where the Group operates or is developing its activities there have been new regulations in favour of new investments and bidding procedures for new plants, for the development of new capacities. Properties, Plants and Equipment The Group owns and leases a large number of office buildings and warehouse spaces throughout Italy and in the foreign countries in which it operates. The Group owns substantially all of the plants, machinery and equipment used to carry out its production activities. As of 30 June 2024, no creditors or other third parties had any significant rights over or in respect of any material part of the property, plants or equipment of the Group. Employees As of 30 June 2024, the ENEL Group employed a total of 60,118 employees, of which 31,584 were employed in Italy and 28,534 were employed abroad. The following table sets forth the location and number of the ENEL Group’s employees as of 30 June 202 4, 30 June 2023, 31 December 2023 and 31 December 2022, as directly extracted from the Group’s accounting records. As of 30 June As of 31 December 2024 2023 2023 2022 Employees in Italy ....................................... 31,584 31,997 31,470 31,642 Employees outside of Italy .......................... 28,534